# PLTL GBNF — grammar for llama.cpp grammar-forced decoding.
#
# Derived directly from `ocaml/parser.mly`. The precedence layers below
# mirror the parser's `%right`/`%left`/`%nonassoc` directives, lowest
# precedence at the top, tightest binding at the bottom:
#
#     <->                                       right-assoc  (iff)
#     ->                                        right-assoc  (impl)
#     |                                         left-assoc   (or)
#     &                                         left-assoc   (and)
#     U  S  W  R  T   (binary temporal)         right-assoc
#     !  X  Y  F  G  O  H   (unary prefix)      bind tightest
#     atoms / true / false / "(" formula ")"    primary
#
# Surface form is canonical: matches what the Rust `Display` impl in
# `rust/src/ast.rs` emits (letter-form operators G/F/X/Y/H/O for unary,
# U/S/W/R/T for binary; `!` for negation; `&`/`|`/`->`/`<->` for
# boolean connectives). The OCaml lexer accepts many other spellings
# ([]p, <>p, henceforth p, \G p, ...), but we constrain generation to
# ONE canonical form to keep the grammar small and the search tight.
#
# Atoms follow `ocaml/lexer.mll`'s `lower_id` rule:
#   lowercase letter, then any of lowercase / digit / underscore.
#
# Binary boolean and binary-temporal nodes are emitted fully
# parenthesized so the output round-trips through
# Display -> OCaml-parse without ambiguity, matching what
# `rust/src/ast.rs::Display for Formula` produces.
#
# Whitespace is permissive: `ws` (zero or more spaces) sits between
# every token. We do NOT include newlines or tabs in the surface set
# because the model isn't asked to pretty-print across lines.
#
# References:
#   * GBNF syntax: https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/blob/master/grammars/README.md
#   * llama.cpp uses BNF-ish: `::=` for rules, `[...]` for char classes,
#     `*`/`+`/`?` for repetition, `|` for alternation.

# Top-level: a complete formula, padded with optional whitespace.
root        ::= ws iff-expr ws

# Layer 1: biconditional (lowest precedence, right-associative).
# Recursion on the RHS gives right-associativity: a <-> b <-> c
# parses as a <-> (b <-> c).
iff-expr    ::= impl-expr (ws "<->" ws iff-expr)?

# Layer 2: implication, right-associative.
impl-expr   ::= or-expr  (ws "->"  ws impl-expr)?

# Layer 3: disjunction, left-associative.
# Left-associativity in GBNF is encoded by recursion on the LHS of the
# tail repetition: head followed by zero or more "| rhs" tails.
or-expr     ::= and-expr (ws "|"   ws and-expr)*

# Layer 4: conjunction, left-associative.
and-expr    ::= bin-temp (ws "&"   ws bin-temp)*

# Layer 5: binary temporal (U, S, W, R, T), right-associative.
# Same RHS recursion trick as iff/impl.
bin-temp    ::= unary-expr (ws bin-temp-op ws bin-temp)?

bin-temp-op ::= "U" | "S" | "W" | "R" | "T"

# Layer 6: unary prefix operators (tightest binding).
# Allowing the chain (`! G F p`) by recursing on `unary-expr` matches
# the OCaml parser, which lets unary tokens stack with no parens.
# NOTE: alternations must stay on ONE line — llama.cpp's GBNF parser
# (verified on build b9860, July 2026) rejects continuation lines that
# start with `|`.
unary-expr  ::= unary-op ws unary-expr | primary

unary-op    ::= "!" | "X" | "Y" | "F" | "G" | "O" | "H"

# Layer 7: primary — atom, boolean constant, or parenthesized formula.
primary     ::= "true" | "false" | atom | "(" ws iff-expr ws ")"

# Atoms — `lower_id` from `ocaml/lexer.mll`:
#   lowercase letter, then lowercase / digit / underscore.
# `true` and `false` are reserved (handled above as constants); GBNF
# can't easily exclude them from the atom rule, but the model is
# heavily biased toward emitting the constants directly by the rule
# order. The OCaml lexer would tokenise `true`/`false` as constants
# anyway, so a model that emits `true` as an atom would still parse
# correctly.
atom        ::= [a-z] [a-z0-9_]*

# Whitespace: zero or more spaces between tokens. We deliberately do
# not allow tabs/newlines — the model is expected to emit a single
# formula on a single line.
ws          ::= [ ]*
